{
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // NOT USED BY THE CURRENT LIVE DEPLOYMENT. Read this whole comment block
  // before touching this file or wiring it into anything.
  //
  // winsvpn.org / winsvpn.com are served today by the Cloudflare **Pages**
  // Direct Upload project named `winsvpn`. The project has no Git provider.
  // An operator publishes a clean staged copy of `web/` with
  // `wrangler pages deploy <directory> --project-name winsvpn --branch main`.
  // Pages supplies the ASSETS binding used by `_worker.js` advanced mode.
  // This migration-target file is deliberately excluded from the uploaded
  // directory and is therefore inert for the current Pages deployment.
  // Current Pages bindings are configured in the Cloudflare dashboard.
  //
  // What this file IS: the documented **migration target** described in
  // the GPT 5.6 audit finding M-9 ("Pages translation/AI quotas are
  // isolate-local and translation remains an open cost proxy") and finding
  // L-8 ("Worker migration rate-limit bindings are incomplete", which added
  // the four payment bindings below so they would not be forgotten if this
  // ever migrates). Cloudflare Rate Limiting bindings (the `ratelimits`
  // block below) are a Workers-only feature -- Cloudflare's Pages config
  // validation rejects that binding type outright, which is exactly why
  // `web/_worker.js`'s `requireQuota()` helper only *optionally* uses a
  // bound limiter (see the comment there) and otherwise falls back to its
  // existing isolate-local rate limiter. That fallback is the live behavior
  // today and this file does not change it.
  //
  // This file becomes relevant only if/when a human operator deliberately
  // migrates this site off Pages onto a plain Cloudflare Worker (Workers
  // now support serving static assets directly, which is what would let
  // `_worker.js`'s `env.ASSETS.fetch(...)` calls keep working outside
  // Pages). Before that migration is ever run for real, a human must still:
  //
  //   1. Provision real Rate Limiting namespaces (Cloudflare dashboard, or
  //      `wrangler ratelimit namespace create`) and replace the
  //      `namespace_id` placeholder strings below with the real IDs. "1001"
  //      and "1002" (translate/AI chat) and "1003" (shared by the four
  //      payment bindings) are non-functional placeholders, not secrets and
  //      not real Cloudflare resource IDs -- real namespace IDs are long
  //      numeric/hex identifiers, so these will not silently pass as real
  //      config. The four payment bindings may keep sharing one namespace
  //      (see the comment above them) or be split into more if ever
  //      desired -- either is safe.
  //   2. Add a static-assets binding (Workers Static Assets, e.g. an
  //      `"assets": { "directory": "./", "binding": "ASSETS" }` block) so
  //      `env.ASSETS.fetch(request)` in `_worker.js` keeps working when this
  //      is deployed as a plain Worker instead of a Pages project -- Pages
  //      provides that binding automatically today; a plain Worker does
  //      not, without this.
  //   3. Re-create the equivalent of `web/_headers` / `web/_redirects`
  //      behavior (Pages-specific conventions this Worker does not
  //      currently replicate on its own for pure static-asset requests).
  //   4. Cut the custom domain over from the Pages project to the new
  //      Worker in the Cloudflare dashboard, and confirm the apex/CNAME
  //      routing notes in README_FINAL_DEPLOYMENT.md still hold.
  //
  // Until that migration happens, per-path abuse protection additionally
  // needs a zone-level WAF Rate Limiting rule for /api/site/translate and
  // /api/site/ai-chat (configured directly in the Cloudflare dashboard --
  // out of scope for any file in this repository), plus a Google Cloud
  // Translation API budget/quota alarm. Both are tracked as human ops
  // follow-ups, not something this file or `_worker.js` can enforce.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  "name": "winsvpn-site-api",
  "main": "_worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-24",
  // Website desktop downloads (later R2 hosting mode).
  // Direct artifacts above the Pages 25 MiB asset cap live in this R2 bucket
  // and are served only by _worker.js serveR2Download(): the Worker derives
  // the key `downloads/sha256/<sha256>` from the deployed
  // releases/downloads.json, checks size (and the R2 SHA-256 checksum when the
  // object carries one), and streams the bytes through a SHA-256 verifier.
  // The bucket is private: no public r2.dev URL and no custom domain.
  // The LIVE Pages project does not read this file: the deploy lane adds the
  // same binding in the dashboard (Workers & Pages > winsvpn > Settings >
  // Bindings > Add > R2 bucket, variable name DOWNLOADS_BUCKET, bucket
  // winsvpn-website-downloads) and redeploys. See
  // docs/runbooks/WEBSITE_DOWNLOAD_R2_PUBLICATION.md. No credentials here.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "DOWNLOADS_BUCKET",
      "bucket_name": "winsvpn-website-downloads"
    }
  ],
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_TRANSLATE",
      "namespace_id": "1001", // PLACEHOLDER -- replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_AI_CHAT",
      "namespace_id": "1002", // PLACEHOLDER -- replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    // L-8 ("Worker migration rate-limit bindings are incomplete"): the four
    // payment routes (handleWalletCreateOrder, handleWalletCapture,
    // handleMoneroCreateOrder, handleCryptoCreateInvoice in _worker.js) each
    // call requireQuota() with one of the binding names below too, but had
    // no migration-target entry here -- so a future Worker migration would
    // leave them silently falling back to the isolate-local map limiter
    // (limit 20 / 60s, matching the "simple" values below) instead of a
    // real bound limiter. All four share one placeholder namespace_id: a
    // Cloudflare Rate Limiting binding's counter is keyed by the string
    // passed to limiter.limit({key}), and every call site below already
    // prefixes that key with its own route (e.g. "wallet-create-order:<ip>"
    // vs "monero-create-order:<ip>"), so sharing a namespace cannot
    // cross-count between routes -- same reasoning the audit gives for why
    // one payment namespace is acceptable here.
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_WALLET_ORDER",
      "namespace_id": "1003", // PLACEHOLDER -- replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_WALLET_CAPTURE",
      "namespace_id": "1003", // PLACEHOLDER -- shares the payment namespace above; replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_MONERO_ORDER",
      "namespace_id": "1003", // PLACEHOLDER -- shares the payment namespace above; replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_CRYPTO_INVOICE",
      "namespace_id": "1003", // PLACEHOLDER -- shares the payment namespace above; replace before ever deploying this config for real.
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_WALLET_CONFIG",
      "namespace_id": "1003",
      "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_CRYPTO_CONFIG",
      "namespace_id": "1003",
      "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER_REDEEM",
      "namespace_id": "1003",
      "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 60 }
    }
  ]
}
